Converting an Object (Entity) to a DTO (Data Transfer Object) without Spring is a common requirement in microservices, legacy systems, or when you want to keep your dependencies minimal.

Here are the four most effective approaches, ranging from manual to automated.

---

### 1. The Manual Approach (Using `BeanUtils`)
This is the most common "Spring-less" way. You use the standard Java library `org.springframework.beans.BeanUtils` (which is just a utility class, not a Spring framework dependency) or write a custom copy method.

**Pros:** Explicit, easy to read, no build-time dependencies.
**Cons:** You must manually map every field. If you add a field to the Entity, you must remember to add it to the DTO.

**Example:**
```java
// 1. Define DTO
public class UserDTO {
